window.docContents[164].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Direct-sum-of-quiver-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Direct-sum-of-quiver-representations--Direct-sum-of-quiver-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Combines two auxiliary quiver-indexed objects over a commutative semiring into one auxiliary object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.8.9; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Direct sum of quiver representations\u0009Direct sum of quiver representations\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Direct-sum-of-quiver-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Direct-sum-of-quiver-representations--Direct-sum-of-quiver-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/#chapter-05___section-5-12":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.12. Representations of $S_n$","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/#chapter-05___section-5-12"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___2":{"contents":"Definition 6.6.2. Let Q be any quiver and let i \\in Q be a sink (respectively, a source). Then we let \\overline{Q}_i be the quiver obtained from Q by reversing all arrows pointing into (respectively, pointing out of) i.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.6. Reflection functors\u0009Definition 6.6.2: Reversed quiver at a vertex","header":"Definition 6.6.2: Reversed quiver at a vertex","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___2___-Reversed-quiver-at-a-vertex/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___2"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Proposition-6___6___5___-Indecomposable-reps-are-surjective-at-sinks-___-injective-at-sources/#Chapter6___Proposition6___6___5":{"contents":"Proposition 6.6.5. Let Q be a quiver and V be an indecomposable representation of Q.\n\n(1) Let i \\in Q be a sink. Then either \\dim V_i = 1, \\dim V_j = 0 for j \\neq i or\n\n\n\\varphi : \\bigoplus_{j \\to i} V_j \\to V_i\n\n\nis surjective.\n\n(2) Let i \\in Q be a source. Then either \\dim V_i = 1, \\dim V_j = 0 for j \\neq i or\n\n\n\\psi : V_i \\to \\bigoplus_{i \\to j} V_j\n\n\nis injective.\n\nProof. (1) Choose a complement W of \\operatorname{Im} \\varphi. Then we get\n\n\nV = \\begin{array}{c} & W \\\\ 0 \\to \\bullet \\leftarrow 0 \\\\ & \\uparrow \\\\ & 0 \\end{array} \\oplus V'.\n\nSince V is indecomposable, one of these summands has to be zero. If the first summand is zero, then \\varphi has to be surjective. If the second summand is zero, then the first one has to be of the desired form, because else we could write it as a direct sum of several objects of the type\n\n\n\\overset{0}{\\bullet} \\to \\overset{1}{\\bullet} \\leftarrow \\overset{0}{\\bullet}\n\n\n\n\\uparrow\n\n\n\n\\overset{0}{\\bullet}\n\n\nwhich is impossible since V was supposed to be indecomposable.\n\n(2) This follows similarly by splitting away the kernel of \\psi. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.6. Reflection functors\u0009Proposition 6.6.5: Indecomposable reps are surjective at sinks / injective at sources","header":"Proposition 6.6.5: Indecomposable reps are surjective at sinks / injective at sources","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Proposition-6___6___5___-Indecomposable-reps-are-surjective-at-sinks-___-injective-at-sources/#Chapter6___Proposition6___6___5"}});